sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design (orchestrator, 2026-11-13, plan A; 2026-11-14 one ongoing thread per role, continued by default, effort per role through T3 provider instances): a turn of a task that runs in a copy of its Project's default Instance. Swamp runs T3's own CLI in the copy as agent; stock T3 accepts only full-access MCP sessions it issues itself, so no T3 session leaves the container. The implementer and the reviewer each keep one T3 thread that later turns continue. 2026-11-14: a question ends the turn: Orbit stores it, then stops the run through the stock T3 server's own RPC, and the answer comes as the next turn.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: task:turn
  %% kind: tasks.copy_turn
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve
  participant N as Task copy<br/>incus exec · T3
  CLI->>GW: task:turn TASK<br/>--role · --thread
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit task:turn SUBTASK --role=implementer --prompt="Run Doctor and commit a note."`
  %%> - `orbit task:turn SUBTASK --role=implementer --prompt="Start over from the brief." --thread=new`
  %%> - `orbit task:turn SUBTASK --role=reviewer --prompt="Review the checked commit." --check=CHECK`
  Note over GW: Admit the turn<br/>owned copy · subtask · role thread
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - a group that owns a task copy, or whose Project has `nested_fleet` on, turns in its copy as `tasks.copy_turn`; every other group takes the task VM turn (`turn.mmd`). A nested-fleet group without a copy refuses here with `tasks.no_workspace`
  %%> - composes the prompt as for a task VM turn: the title, brief and deliverables of the subtask, then the caller's prompt. A reviewer's prompt also names the checked commit
  %%> - an implementer's prompt says how to ask: with the question tool; a question ends the turn, and the answer comes as the next turn
  %%> - a turn that continues a thread whose last turn asked quotes that question, each with its options, before the caller's prompt: the prompt is the answer
  %%> - without `--thread`, the turn continues its role's thread: the implementer's thread of the current subtask, or the group's reviewer thread, when it is a `t3` thread of this copy. A role with no such thread starts one. `--thread=new` starts a new one; `--thread=ID` must name the thread the turn would continue
  %%> - a new thread records the driver `t3`, the copy's Node, the role's copy model (`ORBIT_TASKS_COPY_IMPLEMENTER_MODEL` or `ORBIT_TASKS_COPY_REVIEWER_MODEL`, each `claude-opus-5-5` by default) and the role's effort (`ORBIT_TASKS_IMPLEMENTER_EFFORT`, default `medium`, or `ORBIT_TASKS_REVIEWER_EFFORT`, default `high`). The T3 provider instance of that effort carries it, as `claudeMedium` or `claudeHigh`
  %%> - a continued thread keeps its model and effort: T3 keeps the thread's provider instance
  %%> - a reviewer turn names the subtask's checked candidate with `--check`, as on a task VM; the commit goes into the turn
  %%> - the intent names the copy's container, the T3 command, whether the turn starts or continues its thread, the provider instance and model, the candidate, the 3,600 s bound and a title that names the Orbit thread; no credential and no vault key
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - the group owns its copy and the copy is `active`; otherwise `tasks.no_workspace` or `tasks.copy_not_ready`, and nothing is recorded
  %%> - an implementer or reviewer turn with no caller's model; a consult or `--model` answers `tasks.copy_turn_unsupported`
  %%> - `--thread=ID` names the role's thread that the turn would continue; otherwise `tasks.thread_role_mismatch`
  %%> - a reviewer's check is the subtask's checked candidate; otherwise `tasks.commit_not_checked`
  %%> - the role's copy model is a model name, and the role's effort is `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh` or `max`; otherwise `tasks.copy_turn_model_invalid` or `tasks.copy_turn_effort_invalid`
  %%> - no other Operation of the group runs; otherwise `operation.target_busy`
  %%> - no earlier turn of the group may still run in the copy. A turn is settled when T3 shows its thread with no active run and a reviewer's checkout is restored, clean on its branch at its tip. The latest turn of each thread whose result is not settled answers `tasks.copy_turn_unended` with its Operation in `details.operation_id`, unless T3 in the copy now shows it settled; a check of the copy refuses the same way. A turn that asked and whose run Orbit stopped is settled, so it holds nothing
  GW->>SW: Start one run<br/>operation-task-copy-turn
  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>Operation recorded
  %%> Result:
  %%> - follow the Operation while Swamp runs the turn
  Note over SW: input
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - writes the copy's container, the SSH target and the turn into the private run folder
  SW->>N: Run the turn
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - one Ansible phase on the copy's Node: `incus exec` into the copy, then `runuser -u agent`, non-login, with the agent's groups
  %%> - first one `true` in the copy: a copy the phase cannot reach fails before anything is launched, so its group stays free
  %%> - a continued turn first finds its T3 thread by its title with `t3 thread list`. A list that fails or is not JSON fails with `tasks.copy_turn_failed`; when T3 holds not exactly one thread of the title, the phase fails with `tasks.copy_thread_missing`. Nothing is launched
  %%> - a reviewer turn then requires a clean checkout on a branch, reads the branch's tip and checks the candidate out, detached; a checkout with changes fails with `tasks.copy_tree_dirty` before anything is launched. The detach mark is written as soon as the checkout is detached
  %%> - a new thread: `t3 thread start --provider --model --title --wait --json /srv/workspace -`; a continued one: `t3 thread send --wait --json THREAD -`. The prompt goes on stdin, bounded by `timeout` at 3,600 s
  %%> - `t3 thread list` finds the thread by its title, also when the bound cut the wait; `t3 thread show --json` reads it back. Each read is bounded at 60 s
  %%> - after a reviewer turn, also a failed or cut one, the phase discards what the reviewer changed and checks the branch out again, then reads that the checkout is clean, on its branch, and that the branch is at the tip read before the detach
  %%> - a run that waits for an answer (`--wait` exits 2): first `t3_copy_rpc.mjs read` stores the thread's pending requests, each question with its id, header, text and options exactly as the agent asked them. Only after that evidence is written, `t3_copy_rpc.mjs interrupt` stops that run with T3's `run.interrupt` and waits up to 30 s for it to end. Each is bounded at 60 s; a run that someone answered meanwhile is not stopped
  %%> - the helper runs on the copy's Node.js runtime, from stdin. It speaks the stock T3 server's own WebSocket RPC (`orchestration.getThreadProjection`, `orchestration.dispatchCommand`) with a session from `t3 auth session issue`: read-only for the read, one minute, held only in the helper's memory, revoked before it exits. T3 stays stock
  %%> - writes each evidence file as soon as its command ends: the thread found to continue, the reviewer's checkout, the detach mark, the launch mark, the start's end, the question, the interrupt, the read, the restore; a cut phase still tells whether a turn was launched
  %%> - T3's CLI issues its own full-access session inside the copy and revokes it when it exits; the replies stay in the run folder, never in a log
  Note over N: T3 runs the turn<br/>provider instance · effort
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the copy's T3 settings hold one `claudeAgent` provider instance per effort level, `claudeLow` to `claudeMax`, each with `CLAUDE_CODE_EFFORT_LEVEL` in its environment; the copy's create writes them
  %%> - Claude Code takes that variable over the `--effort` T3 passes, so the thread runs at its recorded effort: verified with Claude Code 2.1.311 and T3 0.1.7
  Note over SW: result
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - maps the run's status: `completed` is done, `waiting` asks for input, any other status fails with `tasks.turn_failed`, a cut wait fails with `tasks.turn_timeout`, a start that T3 refused fails with `tasks.copy_turn_failed`, a thread to continue that T3 does not hold fails with `tasks.copy_thread_missing`
  %%> - a turn that asks keeps its question in the result. Its run stopped, it is `asking_for_input` and over. A question Orbit could not read fails with `tasks.copy_question_unread`, and a run Orbit could not stop with `tasks.copy_question_unstopped`; neither is over
  %%> - names the thread's provider instance and model as T3 shows them
  %%> - a reviewer turn with the detach mark and no restore that reads clean on its branch at its tip fails with `tasks.copy_review_unrestored`, whatever its run did, also when it failed or nothing was launched; the error keeps the run's own error
  %%> - keeps the message text of the thread, all its turns, redacted and bounded to 256 KiB with the newest kept; T3's CLI gives no tool items and no token use
  %%> - records whether the turn is over: nothing was launched, the start printed an end other than a wait for an answer, or T3 lists no thread of this title or lists it in no active state, which a stopped run is not. An unrestored reviewer turn is never over
  Note over SW: cleanup
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - removes the private run folder
  Note over SW: outcome
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - native assert: the turn is done, or it asked and its run stopped
  alt the turn is done or asked
    SW-->>GW: Native terminal result<br/>capture result
    Note over GW: Record the thread<br/>state · question · transcript
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the thread's state and error, T3's thread id as its external id, and the transcript in the Gateway's archive: `task:agent:show` reads it after the copy is gone
    %%> - a turn that asked keeps its question on the thread: `task:agent:list` shows it until the thread's next turn. The answer is the next `task:turn` of the role, or a message to the thread from a T3 client
    %%> - the result names the provider instance, the model and the effort: the effort is the one the provider instance carries
    %%> - token metrics stay null
  else the turn failed or ran past its bound
    SW--xGW: Native failed result<br/>error code
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the thread is `failed` with T3's status as its error, and the transcript T3 had
    %%> - a turn cut by its bound, a cancelled turn or a lost phase may still run in the copy. Orbit stops only a run that asks. The group refuses more turns and checks with `tasks.copy_turn_unended` until T3 in the copy shows the run over and the checkout restored; the work stays in the copy. `task:cancel` still ends it with the copy
  end
  loop until terminal
    CLI->>GW: operation:show<br/>Operation number
  end
